Lesson 28: Partial, Required aur Readonly — Real-world TypeScript
Ye utility types real projects mein bahut kaam aate hain, especially CRUD applications aur API development mein.
1. Partial<T> — Saari properties optional
Maan lo ek product hai:
type Product = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  price: number;
};


Normal object mein teeno properties required hain:
const product: Product = {
  id: 1,
  name: "Laptop",
  price: 50000
};


Lekin jab sirf price update karni ho, tab Partial use kar sakte hain:
type UpdateProduct = Partial<Product>;

const update: UpdateProduct = {
  price: 45000
};


Ab id, name aur price sab optional ho gaye.
Real use: Update API mein sirf changed fields bhejna.
2. Required<T> — Saari properties required
type User = {
  name?: string;
  email?: string;
};

type CompleteUser = Required<User>;

const user: CompleteUser = {
  name: "Rahul",
  email: "rahul@example.com"
};


Required<User> optional properties ko required bana deta hai.
Agar email missing hoga, toh TypeScript error dega.
Real use: Jab kisi object mein saari fields dena compulsory ho.
3. Readonly<T> — Properties ko reassign karne se rokna
type Config = {
  appName: string;
  version: number;
};

const config: Readonly<Config> = {
  appName: "MyApp",
  version: 1
};

console.log(config.appName);

// config.appName = "NewApp"; // Error


Readonly ke through TypeScript properties ko reassign karne se rokta hai.
Dhyan do: Ye compile-time protection hai. Ye object ko runtime par deeply immutable nahi banata.
4. Teeno ka comparison
Utility type	Kya karta hai?	Example use
Partial<T>	Sab optional	Update product
Required<T>	Sab required	Complete user form
Readonly<T>	Reassignment block	Configuration object
5. Real-world CRUD example
type Product = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  price: number;
};

function updateProduct(
  product: Product,
  changes: Partial<Product>
): Product {
  return {
    ...product,
    ...changes
  };
}

const laptop: Product = {
  id: 1,
  name: "Laptop",
  price: 50000
};

const updatedLaptop = updateProduct(laptop, {
  price: 45000
});

console.log(updatedLaptop);


Output:
{
  id: 1,
  name: "Laptop",
  price: 45000
}


Yahan spread operator (...) original product aur changed properties ko merge karta hai.
Important: Agar id update nahi karna chahiye, toh Partial<Product> ke bajay Partial<Omit<Product, "id">> use kar sakte ho.
